      ":question/id": "8fce4c78-70e1-5b77-9d53-6cbbd7dc7e25",
      ":question/problem": "Given $y'(t)=t^{5}-2$, find $y(t)$, the antiderivative of $y'(t)$.\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=t^{5}-2$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(t^{5}-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(t^{5}-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int t^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-2\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\frac{t^{5+1}}{(5+1)}-2\\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}+C \\\\ &= \\frac{t^{6}}{6}-2t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
