      ":question/problem": "Given $y'(t)=5-t^{3}$, find $y(t)$, the antiderivative of $y'(t)$.\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=5-t^{3}$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(5-t^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(5-t^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= 5\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t-\\int t^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= 5\\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}-\\frac{t^{3+1}}{(3+1)}+C \\\\ &= 5t-\\frac{t^{4}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
